<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yakub II of Germiyan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1429">1429</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yakub II of Germiyan was the last bey of the Germiyanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a>. The Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed his lands in 1390, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402 Yakub II was restored. Having no heir, he bequeathed his principality to the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> on his death in 1429.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yakub-ii-of-germiyan.jpg" alt="Yakub II of Germiyan" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatolian_Beyliks,_Germiyan._Ya%27qub_II_b._Sulayman._First_reign,_AH_789-792_(AD_1387-1390)._AR_Ak%C3%A7es_(2)._(17.5mm,_0.86g,_7h;_16.2mm,_0.86g,_12h)._Without_mint-name._Undated._VF,_one_slightly_chipped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yakub II, also known as Yakub Chelebi, was the son of Suleiman Shah, bey of Germiyan, and of a daughter of Umur, ruler of the Aydinids. During his father's reign he was wali, or governor, of Uşak and Şuhut. Suleiman Shah had given numerous towns to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, among them the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a>, as the dowry of his daughter Devletşah Hatun on her marriage to Bayezid, son of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>. Yakub inherited the throne in 1387.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yakub supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a>, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> died there he joined the Karamanids and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> and began retaking the lands of his sister's dowry. When Bayezid seized Saruhan, Aydın and Menteshe, Yakub sent gifts to regain his favour, but he was imprisoned in the castle of Ipsala and Germiyan came wholly under Ottoman control in 1390.</p><p>In 1399 he escaped and, travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in disguise, sought the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a> in 1402 the former troops of Germiyan, Aydın and Menteshe changed sides, and Yakub recognised Bayezid in the fighting and had him captured. During the Ottoman Interregnum he allied with Mehmed Chelebi, which brought war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>, and in 1411 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> of Karaman. Mehmed Chelebi, as Sultan Mehmed I, reinstated Yakub in 1414.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yakub later backed Mustafa Chelebi as a claimant to the Ottoman throne against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, but after Mustafa's defeat and execution he returned to friendly relations with Murad. Having no sons, and unwilling to leave his realm to his sister's children, Yakub bequeathed Germiyan to Murad II. In 1428 he paid his respects at the graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> and formally declared his will before Murad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>. He fell sick after returning home and died in January 1429, and Murad annexed Germiyan.</p><p>His court was a centre of science and literature. The poet Sheikhi Sinan was his companion, and Ahmed-i Dai translated the Persian Tabirname into Turkish at his order. His building complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a>, built in 1411–1412, holds his tomb and that of his wife Pasha Kerime Hatun; restored in 1999, it reopened as the Encaustic Tile Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yakub II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bayezid I took the rest of the beylik in 1390, but Timur restored <b>Yakub II</b> in 1402.</blockquote><a href="/state/germiyanids">Germiyanids</a></li></ul></section>
